---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fichaUAH, urlSitio } from '../../i18n';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base titulo="The project" lang="en">
  <header class="post-header">
    <h1 class="post-title">the project</h1>
    <p class="post-description">Motivation, hypothesis and objectives of MESH.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="linea">
    <h2>why</h2>
    <p>
      Distributed systems underpin much of our everyday infrastructure: traffic management in cities, the Internet of
      Things in agriculture and industry, the cyber-resilience mechanisms of communication networks. Optimising them
      is a formidable engineering problem, and the difficulty is not accidental. It arises from three factors acting
      together: the <strong>scale</strong> of deployments, which rules out exhaustive search; the
      <strong>hidden interdependencies</strong> among their elements, which make many of these problems NP-hard; and
      the <strong>feedback loops</strong> those relationships generate, especially when the elements are autonomous,
      self-interested agents.
    </p>
    <p>
      The state of the art addresses these challenges only in part. Classical metaheuristics such as simulated
      annealing or evolutionary algorithms find good solutions at scale, but do not exploit the relational structure of
      the problem. Distributed optimisation techniques based on message passing and negotiation do exploit it and
      handle self-interested behaviour well, but their scalability is limited.
    </p>
    <p>
      In machine learning, graph neural networks have shown that working directly on the structure of the data makes it
      possible to identify latent relationships and solve previously intractable problems. MESH brings that
      "structural awareness" to the optimisation of distributed systems.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="linea">
    <h2>hypothesis</h2>
    <p>
      Optimisation algorithms that natively integrate and exploit the relationships of the problem, modelled as a
      <strong>multilayer factor graph</strong>, will outperform traditional structure-blind metaheuristics both in
      solution quality and in convergence time. We also want these techniques to behave in an <em>anytime</em>
      fashion and to serve to steer a distributed system towards socially efficient outcomes without ignoring the goals
      of each of its agents.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="linea">
    <h2>objectives</h2>
    <h3>Model distributed systems as multilayer factor graphs</h3>
    <p>
      Develop a formal representation that captures both the network structure of a distributed system and the
      interaction dynamics among its elements, combining multilayer network formulations with the modelling of
      higher-order interactions through factor graphs. The model is enriched automatically: from real or synthetic
      data, graph self-supervised learning techniques identify hidden relationships that are added as new attributes,
      links or layers.
    </p>
    <figure class="figura">
      <img src={`${base}/img/fig-mfg.png`} alt="Diagram of multilayer factor graph modelling for a Wi-Fi scenario: known data generate the access point, interference and alternative association layers, and a predictive model adds the factor graph, other hidden relationships and attribute updates. Labels in Spanish." />
      <figcaption>Multilayer factor graph modelling for a Wi-Fi scenario. The upper layers come from visible data; hidden relationships are extracted with a predictive model. Labels in Spanish.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h3>Bring the expressiveness of the model into metaheuristics</h3>
    <p>
      Adapt the main families of metaheuristic algorithms so that they exploit the relationships expressed in the
      multilayer factor graph: relationship-aware crossover and mutation operators, reduction of the number of
      variables according to their importance in the structure, and decision mechanisms that speed up convergence,
      with metrics that quantify the gain over the original algorithms.
    </p>
    <h3>Reconfigure and optimise the structure through message passing</h3>
    <p>
      Explore distributed methods that, during optimisation itself, detect which structural properties of the graph
      help or hinder reaching good solutions and use them as leverage points to induce changes in the model. We call
      this process multilayer factor graph <strong>reshaping</strong>. It helps overcome intrinsic limitations of
      message passing such as <em>over-squashing</em>, and is combined with graph embedding techniques for large
      scenarios and with negotiation mechanisms that balance individual and collective goals.
    </p>
    <figure class="figura">
      <img src={`${base}/img/fig-reshaping.png`} alt="Conceptual example of reshaping: a node colouring problem, its equivalent factor graph, the energy accumulated in some nodes after several belief propagation iterations, and the solution obtained by removing the two highest-energy nodes." />
      <figcaption>Distributed reshaping on a node colouring problem: original graph, factor graph, energy accumulation and solution after removing the highest-energy nodes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="linea">
    <h2>use cases</h2>
    <p>
      MESH is a non-oriented research project: the model and the techniques are general. To validate them we will
      work with open data from three domains where the team has experience: <strong>wireless communications</strong>
      (Wi-Fi and 5G), urban <strong>road transport</strong> and <strong>cybersecurity</strong>, in particular network
      reconfiguration against attacks. Expected benefits include network self-configuration to withstand cyberattacks
      and reduced energy consumption.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="linea">
    <h2>open science</h2>
    <p>
      Results will be published following the FAIR principles and in open access. Pre-trained models will be
      distributed on <a href="https://huggingface.co/" rel="noopener">Hugging Face</a>, and datasets and stable software
      releases, with DOIs, in the <a href="https://zenodo.org/communities/mesh-uah/" rel="noopener">MESH community on Zenodo</a>, registered on the AI-on-Demand
      platform and integrated into the European Open Science Cloud. A workshop will show how to reproduce the
      experiments of one of the published papers from the released code and data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="linea">
    <h2>impact</h2>
    <p>
      Scalability, interdependencies and self-interested behaviour have always been the three main limitations of
      optimising complex distributed systems. MESH proposes a generic model and a set of distributed mechanisms,
      supported by machine learning, that address them together and close the gap between general-purpose
      metaheuristics and graph-based distributed optimisation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its applications contribute to Sustainable Development Goals 11 (sustainable cities, through less congestion and
      emissions), 7 (affordable and clean energy, through more efficient wireless networks) and 9 (industry,
      innovation and infrastructure, through the cyber-resilience of critical infrastructure). The design of the
      algorithms integrates the transparency and human oversight requirements of the EU AI Act, and the gender
      perspective is applied in bias audits of the data and in cost functions that do not penalise complex mobility
      patterns associated with care work.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="linea">
    <h2>funding</h2>
    <p>
      Grant <strong>PID2025-174033NA-I00</strong> funded by MICIU/AEI/10.13039/501100011033, under the 2025 call for
      Knowledge Generation Projects of the Spanish State Plan for Scientific, Technical and Innovation Research
      2024-2027. Non-oriented research projects, type A. Duration: 36 months, from 1 September 2026 to 31 August
      2029. Amount awarded: €85,000.
      <a href={fichaUAH} rel="noopener">Project entry on the UAH research portal</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="linea" id="citar">
    <h2>acknowledgement and how to cite</h2>
    <p>
      Papers, repositories, presentations and any other output derived from the project should include the
      following acknowledgement:
    </p>
    <pre class="cita">Grant PID2025-174033NA-I00 funded by MICIU/AEI/10.13039/501100011033.</pre>
    <pre class="cita" lang="es">Proyecto PID2025-174033NA-I00 financiado por MICIU/AEI/10.13039/501100011033.</pre>
    <p>To cite the project itself:</p>
    <pre class="cita">de la Hoz, E., de la Cruz Piris, L. et al. (2026–2029). MESH: Multilayer Enhanced Structural metaHeuristics for distributed optimization (PID2025-174033NA-I00). University of Alcalá. {urlSitio}</pre>
    <p class="nota">
      Software, datasets and models have their own citation, with DOI, on the <a href={`${base}/en/recursos`}>resources</a> page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="linea" id="transferencia">
    <h2>technology transfer and contact</h2>
    <p>
      MESH is a knowledge generation project and does not pursue immediate transfer, but its techniques apply directly
      to the configuration of communication networks, traffic management and cyber-resilience. We are interested in
      working with network operators, mobility managers and security operations centres who want to validate these
      methods on their own scenarios. The usual routes are research contracts with the University of Alcalá,
      industry-sponsored chairs and joint projects under national or European calls.
    </p>
    <p>
      Contact: Enrique de la Hoz, principal investigator,
      <a href="mailto:enrique.delahoz@uah.es?subject=MESH%3A%20collaboration">enrique.delahoz@uah.es</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
